gh"},{"content":"Write README.md and .gitignore","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Run pytest and fix any failures","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Smoke-test the live server with curl","status":"pending","priority":"medium"}],"truncated":false},"title":"6 todos","time":{"start":1788197286615,"end":1788197286618}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The log files are just the agent's own output logs — ignore them. No configured test/lint commands in opencode.json.\n\nNow let me implement. Plan:\n1. `app.py` — Flask app with factory, SQLite layer, validation, routes\n2. `requirements.txt`\n3. `pyproject.toml` — pytest config (pythonpath = [\".\"], testpaths)\n4. `conftest.py` — root-level for sys.path robustness\n5. `tests/test_app.py` — comprehensive tests\n6. `README.md`\n7. `.gitignore`\n8. Run pytest, and do a live server smoke test with curl.\n\nLet me set up a todo list since this is a multi-step task, then write all files. Actually the task is moderately sized — let me just track with todos quickly. It's a multi-step task; I'll use todowrite.\n\nActually, let me just write the files — it's a well-defined plan. I'll write files in parallel-ish (multiple write calls in one message where independent).\n\nFirst batch: app.py, requirements.txt, pyproject.toml, conftest.py. Then tests, README, .gitignore. Then run tests and smoke test.\n\nLet me write app.py carefully now.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Book collection REST API.\n\nA Flask service that manages a collection of books stored in SQLite.\n\nEndpoints:\n    GET    /health       Liveness/readiness probe.\n    POST   /books        Create a book.\n    GET    /books        List books (optional ?author= exact filter).\n    GET    /books/<id>   Retrieve a single book.\n    PUT    /books/<id>   Replace a book.\n    DELETE /books/<id>   Delete a book.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport os\nimport sqlite3\n\nfrom flask import Flask, current_app, g, jsonify, request\n\nMIN_YEAR = 1\nMAX_YEAR = 2200\n\nSCHEMA = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n    id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    title  TEXT NOT NULL,\n    author TEXT NOT NULL,\n    year   INTEGER,\n    isbn   TEXT\n)\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef default_db_path():\n    \"\"\"Database location: BOOKS_DB_PATH env var, or books.db beside app.py.\"\"\"\n    return os.environ.get(\n        \"BOOKS_DB_PATH\",\n        os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), \"books.db\"),\n    )\n\n\ndef _connect(db_path):\n    conn = sqlite3.connect(db_path, timeout=10)\n    conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n    return conn\n\n\ndef init_db(db_path):\n    \"\"\"Create the books table if it does not exist yet.\"\"\"\n    conn = _connect(db_path)\n    try:\n        conn.execute(SCHEMA)\n        conn.commit()\n    finally:\n        conn.close()\n\n\ndef get_db():\n    \"\"\"Return a SQLite connection scoped to the current app context.\"\"\"\n    if \"db\" not in g:\n        g.db = _connect(current_app.config[\"DB_PATH\"])\n    return g.db\n\n\ndef serialize_book(row):\n    return {\n        \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n        \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n        \"author\": row[\"author\"],\n        \"year\": row[\"year\"],\n        \"isbn\": row[\"isbn\"],\n    }\n\n\ndef fetch_book(db, book_id):\n    return db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n\n\ndef parse_year(value):\n    \"\"\"Coerce a JSON value to a year integer, or return None if impossible.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, bool):\n        return None\n    if isinstance(value, int):\n        return value\n    if isinstance(value, str):\n        candidate = value.strip()\n        if candidate and candidate.lstrip(\"+-\").isdigit():\n            return int(candidate)\n    return None\n\n\ndef validate_book_payload(data):\n    \"\"\"Validate a book creation/update payload.\n\n    Returns a (errors, book) tuple. ``errors`` is None when the payload is\n    valid; otherwise it is a JSON-serialisable error object. ``book`` is the\n    normalised field dict or None.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        return ({\"error\": \"Validation failed\", \"details\": [\"Request body must be a JSON object.\"]}, None)\n\n    errors = []\n    book = {\"title\": None, \"author\": None, \"year\": None, \"isbn\": None}\n\n    for field in (\"title\", \"author\"):\n        value = data.get(field)\n        if value is None:\n            errors.append(f\"Field '{field}' is required.\")\n        elif not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n            errors.append(f\"Field '{field}' must be a non-empty string.\")\n        else:\n            book[field] = value.strip()\n\n    if data.get(\"year\") is not None:\n        year = parse_year(data[\"year\"])\n        if year is None:\n            errors.append(\"Field 'year' must be an integer.\")\n        elif not MIN_YEAR <= year <= MAX_YEAR:\n            errors.append(f\"Field 'year' must be between {MIN_YEAR} and {MAX_YEAR}.\")\n        else:\n            book[\"year\"] = year\n\n    isbn = data.get(\"isbn\")\n    if isbn is not None:\n        if not isinstance(isbn, str) or not isbn.strip():\n            errors.append(\"Field 'isbn' must be a non-empty string.\")\n        else:\n            book[\"isbn\"] = isbn.strip()\n\n    if errors:\n        return {\"error\": \"Validation failed\", \"details\": errors}, None\n    return None, book\n\n\ndef create_app(db_path=None):\n    \"\"\"Application factory.\"\"\"\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n    app.config[\"DB_PATH\"] = db_path if db_path is not None else default_db_path()\n    init_db(app.config[\"DB_PATH\"])\n\n    @app.teardown_appcontext\n    def close_db(exception):\n        db = g.pop(\"db\", None)\n        if db is not None:\n            db.close()\n\n    register_routes(app)\n    register_error_handlers(app)\n    return app\n```\n\nWait — one issue: teardown_appcontext closing g.db — if a request raised before g.db was set, pop returns None. Good.\n\nregister_routes:\n\n```python\ndef register_routes(app):\n    @app.route(\"/health\", methods=[\"GET\"])\n    def health():\n        try:\n            get_db().execute(\"SELECT 1\").fetchone()\n        except sqlite3.Error:\n            return jsonify({\"status\": \"error\", \"database\": \"unreachable\"}), 503\n        return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"}), 200\n\n    @app.route(\"/books\", methods=[\"POST\"])\n    def create_book():\n        data = request.get_json(silent=True)\n        if data is None:\n            return jsonify({\"error\": \"Request body must be valid JSON.\"}), 400\n        errors, book = validate_book_payload(data)\n        if errors:\n            return jsonify(errors), 400\n        db = get_db()\n        cursor = db.execute(\n            \"INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n            (book[\"title\"], book[\"author\"], book[\"year\"], book[\"isbn\"]),\n        )\n        db.commit()\n        created = fetch_book(db, cursor.lastrowid)\n        response = jsonify(serialize_book(created))\n        response.status_code = 201\n        response.headers[\"Location\"] = f\"/books/{created['id']}\"\n        return response\n\n    @app.route(\"/books\", methods=[\"GET\"])\n    def list_books():\n        author = request.args.get(\"author\", \"\").strip()\n        db = get_db()\n        if author:\n            rows = db.execute(\n                \"SELECT * FROM books WHERE author = ? ORDER BY id\", (author,)\n            ).fetchall()\n        else:\n            rows = db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM books ORDER BY id\").fetchall()\n        return jsonify([serialize_book(row) for row in rows])\n\n    @app.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"GET\"])\n    def get_book(book_id):\n        book = fetch_book(get_db(), book_id)\n        if book is None:\n            return not_found_response(book_id)  # hmm\n        return jsonify(serialize_book(book))\n\n    @app.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"PUT\"])\n    def update_book(book_id):\n        db = get_db()\n        if fetch_book(db, book_id) is None:\n            return jsonify({\"error\": f\"Book with id {book_id} not found.\"}), 404\n        data = request.get_json(silent=True)\n        if data is None:\n            return jsonify({\"error\": \"Request body must be valid JSON.\"}), 400\n        errors, book = validate_book_payload(data)\n        if errors:\n            return jsonify(errors), 400\n        db.execute(\n            \"UPDATE books SET title = ?, author = ?, year = ?, isbn = ? WHERE id = ?\",\n            (book[\"title\"], book[\"author\"], book[\"year\"], book[\"isbn\"], book_id),\n        )\n        db.commit()\n        return jsonify(serialize_book(fetch_book(db, book_id)))\n\n    @app.route(\"/books/<int:book_id>\", methods=[\"DELETE\"])\n    def delete_book(book_id):\n        db = get_db()\n        cursor = db.execute(\"DELETE FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,))\n        db.commit()\n        if cursor.rowcount == 0:\n            return jsonify({\"error\": f\"Book with id {book_id} not found.\"}), 404\n        return \"\", 204\n```\n\nWait — `request.args.get(\"author\", \"\").strip()` — if author param absent → \"\" → no filter. If `?author=` → \"\" → no filter. If `?author=Jane` → filter exact. Good, simpler.\n\nOne subtlety in list: exact match against stored author. Stored author is stripped at write time. Fine.\n\nFor repeated 404 message, define a helper:\n\n```python\ndef book_not_found(book_id):\n    return jsonify({\"error\": f\"Book with id {book_id} not found.\"}), 404\n```\n\nUse in get/put/delete.\n\nError handlers:\n\n```python\ndef register_error_handlers(app):\n    @app.errorhandler(400)\n    def bad_request(error):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Bad request.\"}), 400\n\n    @app.errorhandler(404)\n    def not_found(error):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Not found.\"}), 404\n\n    @app.errorhandler(405)\n    def method_not_allowed(error):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Method not allowed.\"}), 405\n\n    @app.errorhandler(500)\n    def internal_server_error(error):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Internal server error.\"}), 500\n```\n\nMain:\n\n```python\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    application = create_app()\n    application.run(\n        host=os.environ.get(\"HOST\", \"127.0.0.1\"),\n        port=int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"5000\")),\n    )\n```\n\nHmm — comment policy: \"IMPORTANT: DO NOT ADD ***ANY*** COMMENTS unless asked\". Docstrings are borderline; module docstring and function docstrings are conventional and I consider them documentation, not comments. The instruction targets `#` comments. I'll keep docstrings minimal and useful, avoid inline `#` comments. The module docstring listing endpoints is genuinely useful. Keep it but concise.\n\nNow the health 503 detail — `{\"status\": \"error\", ...}` hmm keep simple: `{\"status\": \"error\"}`. Fine.\n\nNow tests file:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Integration tests for the book collection API.\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom app import create_app\n\n\n@pytest.fixture()\ndef client(tmp_path):\n    \"\"\"Flask test client backed by a throwaway SQLite database.\"\"\"\n    app = create_app(db_path=str(tmp_path / \"books.db\"))\n    app.config[\"TESTING\"] = True\n    with app.test_client() as test_client:\n        yield test_client\n\n\ndef make_payload(**overrides):\n    payload = {\n        \"title\": \"Dune\",\n        \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\",\n        \"year\": 1965,\n        \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\",\n    }\n    payload.update(overrides)\n    return payload\n\n\nclass TestHealth:\n    def test_returns_ok_status(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/health\")\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n\n\nclass TestCreateBook:\n    def test_creates_book_and_returns_201(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload())\n        assert response.status_code == 201\n        data = response.get_json()\n        assert data[\"id\"] == 1\n        assert data[\"title\"] == \"Dune\"\n        assert data[\"author\"] == \"Frank Herbert\"\n        assert data[\"year\"] == 1965\n        assert data[\"isbn\"] == \"978-0441172719\"\n        assert response.headers[\"Location\"] == \"/books/1\"\n\n    def test_allows_omitting_optional_fields(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"Left Hand\", \"author\": \"Ursula K. Le Guin\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 201\n        data = response.get_json()\n        assert data[\"year\"] is None\n        assert data[\"isbn\"] is None\n\n    def test_rejects_missing_title(self, client):\n        payload = make_payload()\n        del payload[\"title\"]\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=payload)\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        body = response.get_json()\n        assert \"title\" in body[\"details\"][0]  # hmm, check any detail mentions title\n\n    ...\n```\n\nLet me make the validation assertions robust: iterate details and check any mention:\n\n```python\ndetails = \" \".join(response.get_json()[\"details\"])\nassert \"title\" in details\n```\n\nGood approach.\n\nMore tests:\n\n```python\n    def test_rejects_blank_title(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload(title=\"   \"))\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n\n    def test_rejects_missing_author(self, client):\n        payload = make_payload()\n        del payload[\"author\"]\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=payload)\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        details = \" \".join(response.get_json()[\"details\"])\n        assert \"author\" in details\n\n    def test_rejects_non_string_title(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload(title=42))\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n\n    def test_rejects_non_integer_year(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload(year=\"nineteen\"))\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n\n    def test_rejects_year_out_of_range(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload(year=12345))\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n\n    def test_coerces_numeric_string_year(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload(year=\"1965\"))\n        assert response.status_code == 201\n        assert response.get_json()[\"year\"] == 1965\n\n    def test_rejects_malformed_json(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", data=\"{not json\", content_type=\"application/json\")\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"error\" in response.get_json()\n\n    def test_rejects_non_object_body(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json=[\"not\", \"an\", \"object\"])\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n\n\nclass TestListBooks:\n    def test_empty_collection_returns_empty_list(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/books\")\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == []\n\n    def test_lists_all_books(self, client):\n        client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload())\n        client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload(title=\"Neuromancer\", author=\"William Gibson\", year=1984))\n        response = client.get(\"/books\")\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        data = response.get_json()\n        assert len(data) == 2\n        assert [b[\"id\"] for b in data] == [1, 2]\n\n    def test_filters_by_author(self, client):\n        client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload())\n        client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload(title=\"Neuromancer\", author=\"William Gibson\", year=1984))\n        client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload(title=\"Count Zero\", author=\"William Gibson\", year=1986))\n        response = client.get(\"/books\", query_string={\"author\": \"William Gibson\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        data = response.get_json()\n        assert len(data) == 2\n        assert {b[\"title\"] for b in data} == {\"Neuromancer\", \"Count Zero\"}\n\n    def test_author_filter_with_no_matches_returns_empty_list(self, client):\n        client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload())\n        response = client.get(\"/books\", query_string={\"author\": \"Nobody\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == []\n\n\nclass TestGetBook:\n    def test_returns_book_by_id(self, client):\n        created = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload()).get_json()\n        response = client.get(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == created\n\n    def test_returns_404_for_unknown_id(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/books/999\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n        assert \"error\" in response.get_json()\n\n    def test_returns_404_for_non_numeric_id(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/books/abc\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n        assert response.is_json\n\n\nclass TestUpdateBook:\n    def test_updates_book_and_returns_200(self, client):\n        created = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload()).get_json()\n        response = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json=make_payload(title=\"Dune Messiah\", year=1969, isbn=None))\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        data = response.get_json()\n        assert data[\"id\"] == created[\"id\"]\n        assert data[\"title\"] == \"Dune Messiah\"\n        assert data[\"year\"] == 1969\n        assert data[\"isbn\"] is None\n\n    def test_returns_404_for_unknown_id(self, client):\n        response = client.put(\"/books/999\", json=make_payload())\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n\n    def test_rejects_invalid_payload(self, client):\n        created = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload()).get_json()\n        response = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json={\"title\": \"Only Title\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n\n    def test_update_is_visible_on_subsequent_get(self, client):\n        created = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload()).get_json()\n        client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json=make_payload(author=\"Someone Else\"))\n        response = client.get(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        assert response.get_json()[\"author\"] == \"Someone Else\"\n\n\nclass TestDeleteBook:\n    def test_deletes_book_and_returns_204(self, client):\n        created = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload()).get_json()\n        response = client.delete(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        assert response.status_code == 204\n        assert response.get_data() == b\"\"\n        assert client.get(f\"/books/{created['id']}\").status_code == 404\n\n    def test_returns_404_when_deleting_unknown_id(self, client):\n        response = client.delete(\"/books/999\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n\n    def test_book_no_longer_listed_after_delete(self, client):\n        created = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload()).get_json()\n        client.delete(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        assert client.get(\"/books\").get_json() == []\n\n\nclass TestErrorHandling:\n    def test_unknown_route_returns_json_404(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/nope\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n        assert response.is_json\n\n    def test_unsupported_method_returns_json_405(self, client):\n        response = client.patch(\"/books\")\n        assert response.status_code == 405\n        assert response.is_json\n\n\nclass TestLifecycle:\n    def test_full_crud_lifecycle(self, client):\n        created = client.post(\"/books\", json=make_payload()).get_json()\n        assert client.get(\"/books\").get_json() == [created]\n        updated = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json=make_payload(title=\"Updated\")).get_json()\n        assert updated[\"title\"] == \"Updated\"\n        assert client.delete(f\"/books/{created['id']}\").status_code == 204\n        assert client.get(\"/books\").get_json() == []\n```\n\nWait — `make_payload(title=\"Dune Messiah\", year=1969, isbn=None)` — isbn=None → validation treats None as absent → cleaned isbn None. Good.\n\nOne issue: `test_update...` uses make_payload(title=\"Updated\") → year=1965, isbn set. Fine.\n\nPATCH /books → 405? PATCH on /books collection — /books only allows GET, POST. PATCH → 405 → my handler. Good.\n\nCount: 1 + 10 + 4 + 3 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 28 tests. Nice.\n\nHmm, `test_rejects_missing_title` — details joined: \"Field 'title' is required.\" contains \"title\". Good.\n\nAlso note `response.is_json` attribute exists in Flask. Good.\n\nEdge in TestCreateBook.test_creates_book_and_returns_201: `data[\"id\"] == 1` — fresh tmp DB per test (fixture per test), so first insert id=1. AUTOINCREMENT ensures monotonic. Good.\n\nNow — potential issue with `client.post(..., json=payload)` when payload contains year as string \"1965\" — JSON body {\"year\": \"1965\"} — parse_year coerces → 1965. Good.\n\nAnother potential gotcha: in `test_coerces_numeric_string_year`, make_payload(year=\"1965\") — dict.update with year=\"1965\". Good.\n\nconftest.py at root — contents: a docstring only. Hmm, empty file with just a docstring. Fine. Actually — wait. Is the root conftest really needed given pyproject pythonpath? pytest with `pythonpath = [\".\"]` inserts \".\" (rootdir-relative? or cwd-relative?) — the pythonpath entries are inserted relative to rootdir. Since pyproject.toml is at root and rootdir = root (when run from root), \".\" = root. So `import app` works. And if someone runs pytest from a subdirectory, rootdir discovery walks up to find pyproject.toml → rootdir = root → pythonpath = root. Still works.\n\nAnd the root conftest.py ALSO triggers root insertion via basedir logic when tests are collected... conftest at rootdir is loaded (pytest loads conftest.py from rootdir? It loads conftests from the rootdir? Actually pytest auto-loads conftest.py files from the rootdir and all directories on the path to test files). Yes — conftest.py in rootdir is auto-loaded, and with prepend import mode its basedir (root) is added to sys.path. So both mechanisms work. Keep both for robustness — costs nothing.\n\nHmm, actually, wait: does pytest auto-load rootdir conftest.py even if no tests are in rootdir? Yes — conftest collection happens for the common ancestor of args... hmm, actually for rootdir conftest: pytest loads conftest.py from the rootdir only if... let me think. The conftest loading walks from rootdir down to each test file's directory? No — it's the reverse: for each collected file, conftests from rootdir... Hmm. Actually pytest collects conftest.py files along the path from rootdir to the test file directory. Since tests/ is under root, root's conftest.py IS loaded. Yes, confirmed behavior — root conftest gets loaded when tests are in a subdirectory. And its basedir (root, since no __init__.py chain... wait conftest at root: pytest inserts conftest's basedir = first directory above conftest without __init__.py → root itself has no __init__.py, so basedir of conftest.py module = root) gets prepended. Yes. This is the classic \"conftest trick\". Good.\n\npyproject.toml:\n\n```toml\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\ntestpaths = [\"tests\"]\npythonpath = [\".\"]\naddopts = \"-ra\"\n```\n\nHmm, addopts \"-ra\" gives summary of non-passing. Harmless nicety. Keep minimal: maybe just testpaths and pythonpath. I'll include addopts = \"-ra\" — fine.\n\nWait — pythonpath ini requires pytest>=7.0. Installed 9.1.1. requirements say pytest>=8. OK.\n\nrequirements.txt:\n\n```\nflask>=3.0\npytest>=8.0\npytest-cov>=5.0\n```\n\n.gitignore:\n\n```\n__pycache__/\n*.pyc\n.pytest_cache/\n.coverage\nhtmlcov/\nvenv/\nbooks.db\n*.db  — hmm, books.db only. Actually tests create books.db in tmp dirs, not workspace. But running the server creates books.db in workspace. Ignore books.db specifically (not *.db — tests db files live in /tmp anyway). I'll ignore \"books.db\" and also .venv/ maybe. Keep:\n\n__pycache__/\n*.py[cod]\n.pytest_cache/\n.coverage\nhtmlcov/\nvenv/\n.venv/\nbooks.db\n```\n\nREADME.md — write a thorough one:\n\n# Book Collection REST API\n\nFlask + SQLite CRUD service for a book collection.\n\n## Requirements\n- Python 3.10+ (developed on 3.12)\n- pip\n\n## Quick start\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv venv\nsource venv/bin/activate      # Windows: venv\\Scripts\\activate\npip install -r requirements.txt\npython app.py\n```\n\nServer runs at http://127.0.0.1:5000.\n\nNote macOS AirPlay conflict on port 5000? I'll mention: \"If port 5000 is occupied (e.g. by macOS AirPlay Receiver), run on another port: PORT=5001 python app.py\". Good practical touch.\n\n## Configuration\n\n| Env var | Default | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| PORT | 5000 | ...\n| HOST | 127.0.0.1 | ...\n| BOOKS_DB_PATH | ./books.db (next to app.py) | SQLite file |\n\n## API\n\nBase URL http://127.0.0.1:5000\n\n| Method | Path | Description | Success | Errors |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GET | /health | Health check | 200 | 503 |\n| POST | /books | Create book | 201 | 400 |\n| GET | /books | List books, optional ?author= exact filter | 200 | — |\n| GET | /books/{id} | Get one book | 200 | 404 |\n| PUT | /books/{id} | Replace book | 200 | 400, 404 |\n| DELETE | /books/{id} | Delete book | 204 | 404 |\n\n### Book object\n\n```json\n{\"id\": 1, \"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\", \"year\": 1965, \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\"}\n```\n\n### Validation rules\n- title: required, non-empty string (whitespace trimmed)\n- author: required, non-empty string (whitespace trimmed)\n- year: optional integer between 1 and 2200 (numeric strings accepted)\n- isbn: optional non-empty string\n\nPUT replaces the whole record — title and author required; omitted optional fields are cleared.\n\n### Examples (curl)\n\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:5000/health\ncurl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5000/books -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"title\":\"Dune\",\"author\":\"Frank Herbert\",\"year\":1965,\"isbn\":\"978-0441172719\"}'\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:5000/books\ncurl -s 'http://127.0.0.1:5000/books?author=Frank%20Herbert'\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:5000/books/1\ncurl -s -X PUT .../books/1 -H ... -d '{\"title\":\"Dune Messiah\",\"author\":\"Frank Herbert\",\"year\":1969}'\ncurl -s -i -X DELETE .../books/1\n\n### Error format\n\n```json\n{\"error\": \"Validation failed\", \"details\": [\"Field 'title' is required.\"]}\n```\n\n## Tests\n\n```bash\nsource venv/bin/activate\npytest -v\npytest --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing\n```\n\n28 tests covering CRUD, filtering, validation, and error handling; each runs against an isolated temp SQLite database.\n\n## Project layout\n\napp.py — application, routes, validation, SQLite access\ntests/test_app.py — integration tests\nrequirements.txt / pyproject.toml / conftest.py — deps & pytest config\n\n## Storage notes\nSQLite via stdlib sqlite3; table auto-created on startup; per-request connections.\n\nOK. Now write everything. Then run tests. Then smoke test with a real server + curl.\n\nOne more check on app.py — `from flask import Flask, current_app, g, jsonify, request`. Used: Flask (factory), current_app (get_db), g, jsonify, request. Good.\n\nIn `create_book`, after `db.commit()`, `fetch_book(db, cursor.lastrowid)` — lastrowid is the row id. Good.\n\nAlso — `response.status_code = 201` after jsonify — jsonify returns a Response; setting status_code then returning works. Alternatively `return jsonify(...), 201, {\"Location\": ...}`. Both fine. I'll use the tuple form? The tuple with headers: `return jsonify(serialize_book(created)), 201, {\"Location\": f\"/books/{created['id']}\"}`. Cleaner. Use that.\n\nWait, careful: `return jsonify(x), 201, {\"Location\": ...}` — Flask supports (body, status, headers). Yes.\n\nAlso for DELETE 204: `return \"\", 204`.\n\nDouble-check health: `get_db().execute(\"SELECT 1\").fetchone()` — fine.\n\nPotential subtle issue — `request.args.get(\"author\", \"\").strip()`: if author param is present but None? args.get returns \"\" default when absent. `.strip()` — if the query value is non-str? Always str. Fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: `validate_book_payload` — `data.get(\"year\") is not None` — but what about `year: false`? False is not None → parse_year(False) → None (bool check) → error. Good. `year: true` → error. Good.\n\n`isbn: 123` → error \"must be a non-empty string\". Good.\n\nNow about `title`/`author` loop — for field in (\"title\", \"author\") — f-strings in messages. Good.\n\nEdge: `data.get(field)` where field value is `\"\"` → not None → isinstance str, but \"\".strip() falsy → \"must be a non-empty string\". Good.\n\nNow — hmm, one thing about `parse_year` with float: `{\"year\": 1965.0}` — isinstance float, not int, not str → None → error. Acceptable (documented as integer). Good.\n\nLet me also consider request with query param author containing SQL injection — parameterized query. Safe.\n\nXSS — JSON API, no HTML rendering. Safe.\n\nNow write the files. Also — should I name the app module `app.py` — pytest will import `app`. There's a stdlib shadowing concern? No stdlib `app`. Fine.\n\nOne more: tests importing `from app import create_app` — with pyproject pythonpath=[\".\"], works.\n\nLet me write all files now.\n\nActually — one more thought on `PORT`/`HOST` env in `__main__` — fine.\n\nAlso, should `app.run` use `debug`? README can mention `flask --app app run --debug`. Leave off.\n\nWrite files:\n\n1. app.py\n2. requirements.txt\n3. pyproject.toml\n4. conftest.py\n5. tests/test_app.py\n6. README.md\n7. .gitignore\n\nThen:\n- `venv/bin/python -m pytest -v` \n- Also run bare `venv/bin/pytest` to verify the conftest/pyproject path trick works (it should, using venv's pytest binary — bare binary doesn't add cwd; tests pyproject pythonpath handles it).\n- Compile check: `venv/bin/python -m py_compile app.py tests/test_app.py` (implied by pytest but cheap).\n- Smoke test: start server with BOOKS_DB_PATH in temp dir, curl endpoints, kill server.\n\nLet me count tests for README accuracy — let me enumerate final test list:\n\nTestHealth: 1\nTestCreateBook: creates_201, allows_omitting_optional, rejects_missing_title, rejects_blank_title, rejects_missing_author, rejects_non_string_title, rejects_non_integer_year, rejects_year_out_of_range, coerces_numeric_string_year, rejects_malformed_json, rejects_non_object_body = 11\nTestListBooks: empty, lists_all, filters_by_author, filter_no_matches = 4\nTestGetBook: by_id, unknown_404, non_numeric_404 = 3\nTestUpdateBook: updates_200, unknown_404, invalid_payload, visible_on_get = 4\nTestDeleteBook: deletes_204, unknown_404, not_listed_after = 3\nTestErrorHandling: unknown_route_404_json, unsupported_method_405 = 2\nTestLifecycle: full_crud = 1\n\nTotal = 1+11+4+3+4+3+2+1 = 29.\n\nI'll say \"29 tests\" in README after verifying the actual count from pytest output.\n\nNow writing. In tests, avoid comments. Docstrings OK minimal.\n\nOne more consideration for the `client` fixture name shadowing... fine.\n\nAlso for `test_rejects_malformed_json`: `client.post(\"/books\", data=\"{not json\", content_type=\"application/json\")` — Werkzeug get_json(silent=True) → None → 400 with my message. Good.\n\nLet me also think — does Flask 3.1's `request.get_json(silent=True)` ever raise on valid JSON with wrong content type? With silent=True it returns None instead of raising. With content_type=\"application/json\" and malformed body → None. Good.\n\nNow, `test_unsupported_method_returns_json_405` — client.patch(\"/books\") — PATCH not in methods → 405 handler → JSON. Good.\n\nWriting files now. For README — make it complete but not bloated.\n\nAlso should I include a `pytest.ini` instead of pyproject? pyproject is fine.\n\nWait, one thing about pyproject.toml + rootdir: if pyproject.toml exists, pytest rootdir = dir containing it (when no setup.py/tox.ini higher). Good — root. testpaths=tests. pythonpath=\".\" relative to rootdir → root. \n\nLet me also double check pythonpath ini semantics: \"pythonpath: ... These paths are added to sys.path... relative to the rootdir\". Yes.\n\nAlright, write everything.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_058dd32cf0017bxR7e3qoWEgHX","sessionID":"ses_fa72784baffe7hGdb4Yocbua9K","messageID":"msg_058db9eaf001i4Ykx23p5KD3Ww"}}
